          "id": "ch-1-what-is-ai-history-four-perspectives-evolution",
          "number": "1",
          "title": "What is AI? History, Four Perspectives & Evolution",
          "markdown": "## 1. What is AI? History, Four Perspectives & Evolution\n\n> **Exam Definition:** Artificial Intelligence (AI) is the branch of computer science and engineering concerned with designing computational systems (rational agents) that perceive their environment through sensors, reason over knowledge, learn from experience, and execute actions that maximize their chances of achieving stated goals.\n\nWhen students hear the phrase \"Artificial Intelligence,\" they often picture talking robots from science fiction movies. In real engineering, however, AI is much quieter and far more mathematical: it is any computer program that handles tasks we normally assume require human judgment—such as understanding spoken Hindi or English, spotting a hairline tumor in an X-ray, routing delivery trucks around traffic jams, or beating a grandmaster at chess. Early software followed rigid `if-else` instructions written line by line by a human programmer. AI differs fundamentally because an AI system evaluates messy inputs, handles trade-offs, searches through possibilities, or learns patterns directly from data instead of relying on hardcoded answers for every single situation.\n\nClassical AI frameworks organize definitions of AI along two dimensions: *thought vs. behavior*, and *human fidelity vs. ideal rationality*. This produces **four classic schools of thought**. First, **Thinking Humanly** (Cognitive Modeling) tries to uncover how human brains actually process memory and thought through psychological experiments and build software matching those inner steps. Second, **Acting Humanly** (the **Turing Test** approach proposed by Alan Turing in 1950) judges intelligence purely by external conversation: if a human interrogator typing messages to a hidden partner cannot reliably distinguish the machine from a person, the machine passes. To pass the Total Turing Test, a system needs Natural Language Processing, Knowledge Representation, Automated Reasoning, Machine Learning, Computer Vision, and Robotics.\n\nThe third view, **Thinking Rationally** (the \"Laws of Thought\"), traces back to Aristotle's formal logic syllogisms—trying to represent every real-world fact in strict logic and draw mathematically guaranteed conclusions. While clean on paper, this struggles when facts are noisy or missing (\"will it rain in Patna at 4 PM?\"). That brings us to **Acting Rationally** (the **Rational Agent Approach**), which is the dominant framework in modern engineering AI. Rather than copying human mistakes or waiting for 100% logical certainty, a rational agent simply takes the best available action that maximizes its expected performance given whatever sensors and knowledge it currently possesses.\n\n**Real-Life Example:** Consider an aircraft autopilot landing in thick fog. You do *not* want it \"acting humanly\" (getting panicked, fatigued, or slow to react like an exhausted human pilot). You want it **acting rationally**: reading radar altitude, wind shear, and runway alignment sensors every millisecond to touch down safely in the center of the runway.\n\n**Why It Matters:** Clarifying these four perspectives prevents confusion in both system design and exams. Knowing that modern AI builds *rational agents* rather than literal *digital humans* explains why probability, utility, and search dominate modern AI engineering.\n\n### History & Evolution Timeline (1943–Present)\n* **1943 (McCulloch & Pitts):** First mathematical threshold model of an artificial neuron, proving neural nets could compute logical functions (`AND`, `OR`, `NOT`).\n* **1950 (Alan Turing):** Published *\"Computing Machinery and Intelligence\"* introducing the **Turing Test** (Imitation Game) and predicting machine learning.\n* **1956 (Birth of AI — Dartmouth Conference):** **John McCarthy** coined the term *\"Artificial Intelligence\"* during a summer workshop at Dartmouth College alongside Marvin Minsky, Claude Shannon, and Nathaniel Rochester.\n* **1956–1973 (Early Enthusiasm):** Logic Theorist (Newell & Simon), General Problem Solver, Arthur Samuel's self-learning Checkers program, and John McCarthy's LISP language (`1958`).\n* **1974–1980 (First AI Winter):** Over-ambitious promises collided with slow hardware, tiny memory, and *combinatorial explosion* (exponential growth in search problems), causing funding cuts.\n* **1980–1987 (Expert Systems Boom):** Knowledge-rich rule-based systems like **DENDRAL** (chemistry) and **R1/XCON** (computer configuration) solved narrow industrial tasks profitably.\n* **1987–1993 (Second AI Winter):** Maintaining thousands of brittle hand-written `if-then` rules became too expensive, and specialized AI hardware markets collapsed.\n* **1997–2011 (Statistical & Agent Era):** IBM **Deep Blue** defeated Garry Kasparov (`1997`); probabilistic Bayesian networks, SVMs, and IBM Watson (`2011`) matured.\n* **2012–Present (Deep Learning & Generative AI):** GPU acceleration ignited ImageNet breakthroughs (AlexNet `2012`), AlphaGo (`2016`), and Transformer Large Language Models.\n\n| AI Perspective | Focus | Benchmark | Real-World Strength / Limitation |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Thinking Humanly** | Internal brain/mind process | Cognitive psychology | Reveals human reasoning, hard to measure inside black-box software |\n| **Acting Humanly** | Human-like conversation & behavior | Turing Test (`1950`) | Encourages mimicry, but copying human flaws isn't optimal |\n| **Thinking Rationally** | Pure deductive logic rules | Formal syllogisms | Fails under incomplete or noisy sensor information |\n| **Acting Rationally** | Optimal action for highest expected goal | Rational Agent Utility | **Modern standard** — works even with uncertainty and real-time noise |"
