          "id": "ch-3-intelligent-agents-environment-interaction",
          "number": "3",
          "title": "Intelligent Agents & Environment Interaction",
          "markdown": "## 3. Intelligent Agents & Environment Interaction\n\n> **Exam Definition:** An **Agent** is anything that perceives its environment through **Sensors** and acts upon that environment through **Actuators**. A **Rational Agent** selects the action expected to maximize its **Performance Measure**, given the entire percept sequence observed so far and any built-in prior knowledge.\n\nImagine an intelligent system separated cleanly from the outside world by an interface boundary. On the input side, **Sensors** convert physical or digital reality into raw signals (**percepts**) that the software can read. For a self-driving car, cameras, LiDAR, radar, and GPS are sensors; for a spam filter, incoming email headers and text bytes are sensors. The complete history of everything the agent has ever perceived up to the current moment is called its **Percept Sequence**.\n\nInside the software sits the **Agent Program**, which implements a mathematical **Agent Function** mapping percept histories to chosen actions. On the output side, **Actuators** carry out those chosen decisions back upon the world—turning a steering wheel, applying hydraulic brakes, moving a robotic gripper, displaying text on a screen, or moving an email into the Junk folder. Notice that **rationality is not omniscience** (knowing the future): a rational student bringing an umbrella when dark clouds cover 90% of the sky acted rationally even if the wind happens to blow the storm away!\n\nTo design any AI agent cleanly before writing a single line of code, engineers specify the task environment using the **PEAS Framework**:\n* **P — Performance Measure:** Objective criteria defining success (never subjective guesses).\n* **E — Environment:** The external world the agent operates within.\n* **A — Actuators:** Tools through which the agent creates physical or software effects.\n* **S — Sensors:** Devices or inputs through which the agent observes the environment.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  Env[\"Environment\"] -->|\"Percepts\"| Sens[\"Sensors\"]\n  Sens --> Prog[\"Agent Program (Knowledge + Decision)\"]\n  Prog -->|\"Chosen Action\"| Act[\"Actuators\"]\n  Act -->|\"Physical / Digital Effect\"| Env\n```\n\n**Real-Life Example (PEAS for Automated Medical Diagnosis System):**\n* **Performance Measure:** Healthy patient recovery, minimized diagnostic error, low treatment cost, zero missed emergencies.\n* **Environment:** Hospital OPD/ICU, patient symptoms, medical staff, lab test queues.\n* **Actuators:** Screen output showing probable diseases, recommended lab tests, prescribed medications, urgent nurse alert.\n* **Sensors:** Keyboard symptom entry, EHR database records, blood panel values, X-ray/MRI images, pulse oximeter telemetry.\n\n**Why It Matters:** Ambiguous AI projects fail because teams disagree on what \"good\" means. Defining PEAS forces clear performance metrics, accurate hardware/API planning, and realistic environment boundaries from Day 1."
