          "title": "Problem Formulation & State-Space Representation",
          "markdown": "## 1. Problem Formulation & State-Space Representation\n\n> **Exam Definition:** A **Search Problem** is formally defined by five components: an **Initial State**, a set of available **Actions**, a **Transition Model** returning the next state, a **Goal Test**, and an additive **Path Cost Function**. The entire collection of states linked by legal transitions forms the **State Space Graph**.\n\nImagine trying to guide an unfamiliar visitor from Patna Junction to your college gate using phone instructions. Before walking a single step, both of you implicitly agree on the starting landmark, which turns are legal roads (one-way vs. two-way), how to recognize when you have finally reached the college gate, and whether you care about minimizing walking distance or bus fare. Problem-solving agents perform that exact step digitally before running any search algorithm—this crucial design phase is called **Problem Formulation**.\n\nA **State** captures every detail of the world relevant to decision-making, while deliberately dropping useless clutter (**abstraction**). For instance, when finding a driving route across Bihar, our state only tracks which city or intersection you sit in—not the color of the car or what song is playing on the radio! Every legal move transforms one state into a successor via `RESULT(s, a)`, building a directed graph (or expanded **Search Tree** starting from the Initial State). An **Uninformed (Blind) Search** explores this graph with zero clues about how far any node sits from the goal, whereas an **Informed (Heuristic) Search** uses an extra distance-estimation clue $h(n)$ to aim straight toward the target.\n\n**Real-Life Example (8-Puzzle & Route Planning):** In an 8-puzzle sliding tile board, the initial state is the scrambled $3\\times 3$ tile grid, actions slide the blank square `{Up, Down, Left, Right}`, each step costs `1`, and the goal test checks if tiles match `[1..8, blank]`. There are $9!/2 = 181{,}440$ reachable states forming its state space.\n\n**Why It Matters:** Poor state formulation explodes memory or misses solutions entirely; clean abstraction turns intimidating physical problems into clean graphs any standard search algorithm can solve.\n\n### Common Benchmark Graph Used Across All Unit 2 Algorithm Traces\nEvery graph search trace below (`BFS`, `DFS`, `UCS`, `Greedy`, `A*`) runs on this **weighted reference graph** starting at **`S`** with Goal **`G`**:\n* Edges `(from -> to, step cost)`:\n  `S->A (1)`, `S->B (4)`, `A->C (2)`, `A->D (5)`, `B->D (1)`, `B->E (3)`, `C->G (5)`, `D->G (2)`, `E->G (1)`.\n* Heuristic estimates to Goal `G`: `h(S)=6, h(A)=5, h(B)=3, h(C)=4, h(D)=2, h(E)=1, h(G)=0`.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  S[\"S (h=6)\"] -->|\"1\"| A[\"A (h=5)\"]\n  S -->|\"4\"| B[\"B (h=3)\"]\n  A -->|\"2\"| C[\"C (h=4)\"]\n  A -->|\"5\"| D[\"D (h=2)\"]\n  B -->|\"1\"| D\n  B -->|\"3\"| E[\"E (h=1)\"]\n  C -->|\"5\"| G[\"G (h=0) GOAL\"]\n  D -->|\"2\"| G\n  E -->|\"1\"| G\n```"
